fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1131", description: some(value: "A ONE-FIELD NAMED TUPLE IS WRITABLE AS A TYPE BUT NOT AS A VALUE — so an operation can declare a parameter no caller can construct an argument for.\n\nMEASURED on the current tree (probes, since removed):\n  * `operation f(t: (a: Int64)) -> Int64 = t.a` — LOADS. The 1-field named tuple TYPE is fine.\n  * `let t = (a: 1)` — PARSE ERROR: \"syntax error near `a: 1`\". So is `g((a: 1))` as a direct argument.\n  * `let t = (a: 1,)` (trailing comma) — PARSE ERROR, but a DIFFERENT and misleading one: \"tuple literal cannot mix positional and named arguments\" plus \"missing `name`\". Nothing positional was written; tree-sitter error-recovered into the 2+ branch with a MISSING node and the converter's all-or-nothing check then fired on the wreckage.\n  * CONTROL: `let t = (a: 1, b: 2)` then `t.a` — Int(1). Two fields work.\n  * The documented single-member distribute-dot `prow.(name)` LOADS, and per kernel-language section 6.8 it 1-COLLAPSES to `prow.name` — a scalar. So it is not a way to build a 1-field tuple either. There is no way to build one.\n\nCAUSE, located: tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js:1404 —\n    tuple_literal: prec(-2, choice(\n      seq('(', ')'),                                                    // unit\n      seq('(', _fn_arg, ',', commaSep1(_fn_arg), optional(','), ')'),   // 2+ elements\n    ))\nwith `paren_expr: seq('(', _term, ')')` beside it, commented \"Grouping only, no tuple. Distinguished from tuple_literal by absence of comma.\" The 2+ requirement is CORRECT for the POSITIONAL case and is why it exists: `(1)` must be grouping, not a 1-tuple, and the comma is what tells them apart. But for a NAMED single element the ambiguity does not exist — `a: 1` is not a `_term`, so `(a: 1)` can never be a parenthesized expression. The restriction is over-broad for the named case, and that over-breadth is what produces an unconstructible type.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH FIXING RATHER THAN DOCUMENTING AWAY (though see the alternative below). It is not a curiosity: a single-entry name-keyed map is exactly the shape a caller needs whenever an API takes a record of column-to-something, and it is the case those APIs are used for most often. It is what killed one of WI-731's candidate designs for the relation `rename` operator — a map argument written as a tuple literal has no spelling at arity one, which is precisely the single-column rename the ticket exists for.\n\nFIRST TASK — DECIDE, DO NOT ASSUME. Probe whether adding a named-only single-element alternative to `tuple_literal` is conflict-free, the way proposal 056 probed the `...` marker before adopting it. The likely rival is a parenthesized TYPED BINDER in expression position (`lambda (a: Int64) -> ...`), so the probe must cover lambda parameters, and the full corpus must be re-measured, not just the new case. If it is conflict-free, admit it. If it is NOT, then the restriction is a real language rule and must be WRITTEN DOWN — kernel-language section 4.5 states the named-tuple value form and says nothing about a minimum arity — together with a diagnostic that names the actual rule at the actual span, and a note on the type side that such a parameter cannot be given a literal argument.\n\nEITHER WAY, THE TRAILING-COMMA DIAGNOSTIC IS A SEPARATE DEFECT AND MUST GO: \"tuple literal cannot mix positional and named arguments\" is a false statement about a program that mixed nothing. It comes from parse/convert.rs:2043 running on an error-recovered node. Whatever is decided above, `(a: 1,)` must report the real fault at the real span.\n\nACCEPTANCE: either `let t = (a: 1)` parses and `t.a` DRIVES to 1 and a 1-field tuple is passable to a parameter declared `(a: Int64)` — with `(1)` asserted to STAY grouping as the control, since that is the ambiguity the arity rule exists for, and the tree-sitter corpus extended and fully re-measured — OR the refusal is deliberate, recorded in kernel-language section 4.5 with its reason, and reported by a diagnostic that names the rule rather than a fault the author did not commit; in both outcomes `(a: 1,)` no longer claims a positional/named mix; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test"), ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
